  "scraped_at": null,
  "content": {
    "title": "These fruits and vegetables found to have most, least pesticides",
    "text": "March 24, 2026, 7:05 p.m. ETMany of the fruits and vegetables you eat carry a concerning amount of pesticides, a new analysis suggests.The Environmental Working Group (EWG), a Washington, DC-based nonprofit, released its produce report listing the \"Dirty Dozen\", or foods found with the most pesticides and forever chemicals, and the \"Clean Fifteen,\" a list of produce with the lowest amounts of pesticide residue.More than one-third of non-organic, or conventionally grown, fruit and vegetable samples tested positive for at least one of 31 pesticides considered PFAS compounds, also called “forever chemicals” because they don't break down easily, remain in the environment and can build up in the body. Peaches, plums, strawberries and grapes had the most PFAS pesticide residues, while produce such as sweet corn and mushrooms had the least, according to the EWG report.The EWG used U.S. Department of Agriculture testing data on 47 types of produce to create its annual report. The most commonly detected chemical was fludioxonil.More than half of the USDA's samples of peaches, plums, nectarines and pears had residues of fludioxonil, which may harm the liver, hormones and nervous system, the EWG said based on findings from animal studies.Fludioxonil, a chemical applied after harvest to help prevent mold, is considered a forever chemical by some researchers. But the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency does not consider it among PFAS compounds.This represents \"a glaring hole in pesticide oversight,” the EWG said earlier this month when it released a similar report on California-grown produce and pesticides, reported The Palm Springs Desert Sun, part of the USA TODAY Network.Early research suggests some PFAS chemicals may accumulate in crops and pose potential reproductive and developmental risks, although \"significant data gaps remain,\" according to the EWG news release. Exposure to a combination of PFAS and pesticide compounds may be more harmful than a single chemical, some research in animals suggests, the group said.“Consumers have a right to know what’s on their food,” said Varun Subramaniam, a science analyst at the Environmental Working Group. “This year’s findings underscore the presence of PFAS pesticides in the food supply. At the same time, the guide shows there are simple steps shoppers can take to reduce exposure while still eating plenty of fruits and vegetables.”The 'Dirty Dozen' produce found with the most pesticide detectionsThe EWG report found nearly all (96%) of these fruits and vegetables samples contained pesticides and most items had an average of four or more pesticides per sample: SpinachKale, collard and mustard greensStrawberriesGrapesNectarinesPeachesCherriesApplesBlackberriesPearsPotatoesBlueberriesAdditional vegetables found to have high pesticide residue included green beans, and bell and hot peppers.'The Clean Fifteen,' the fruits and vegetables found to be safestThese fruits and vegetables were found to have the least amount of pesticide residue and nearly 60% had no detectable pesticide residues:PineappleSweet cornAvocadosPapayaOnionsSweet peasAsparagusCabbageCauliflowerWatermelonMangoesBananasCarrotsMushroomsKiwiSafety tips for avoiding pesticides in produceThe EWG recommends consumers use these safety measures to reduce exposure to pesticides and chemicals:Buy organic versions of Dirty Dozen produce when possible.Choose more produce from the Clean Fifteen.Buy frozen produce if possible.Wash all fruits and vegetables thoroughly before eating.“A diet rich in fruits and vegetables is essential,” said Dayna de Montagnac, an associate scientist with EWG. “The Shopper’s Guide empowers families to continue enjoying these significant health benefits while making informed choices to reduce pesticide exposure, particularly for children, without sacrificing nutrition.”Contributing: James Ward, The Palm Springs Desert SunMike Snider is a national trending news reporter for USA TODAY. You can follow him on Threads, Bluesky, X and email him at mikegsnider & @mikegsnider.bsky.social & @mikesnider & msnider@usatoday.com.",
